What physical quantities determine the variation of these forces and moments? The answer can be found from the powerful method of dimensional analysis, which is introduced in this section.3 Consider a body of given shape at a given angle of attack (e.g., the airfoil sketched in Figure 1.17). The resultant aerodynamic force is R. On a physical, intuitive basis, we expect R to depend on:
